WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.530
JASON LIU: Once Spark
landed, with the speed

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:05.700
that Cerebras gives you,
it's become my daily driver.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.420
SARAH CHIENG: It feels
like an infomercial.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.480
Codex Spark is a lighter,
faster version of Codex running

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.650
on Cerebras Inference.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:14.100
JASON LIU: Our models

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.720
on Cerebras are going
to get smarter.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.720
The speed is already there, and
the intelligence is catching up.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.950
SARAH CHIENG: Today, we
want to show you how

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.230
to actually take advantage of
that speed with some demos

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.850
that you can build
yourself in the app.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:24.150
JASON LIU: Great.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:24.990
let's get into it.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:29.010
So the first automation I made
when I just joined OpenAI was

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.080
to just understand what's
going on in Slack.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.210
And so we've built
some automations here

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.390
that basically checks upon
Slack, figures out what kind

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:37.590
of topics are happening,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.210
what kind of product
launches are arriving,

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.670
to tell me what's going on.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.950
Obviously, we can turn this
to, you know, every day,

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.250
but with Codex Spark, these
tasks run incredibly fast.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:48.140
One of the great things
about Codex Spark is

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.090
that we can define multi-agents.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.460
And so, maybe the main
agent is responsible

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.050
for understanding what my
goals are for the day.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.750
It can kick off sub-agents
that are fast Spark agents

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.110
to read all of Slack or
Google Drive or Google Meets,

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:02.550
and really help me
understand what's going

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.710
on within the company and
what my responsibilities are.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:05.520
SARAH CHIENG: No,
this is super cool.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:06.210
I love it.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.680
JASON LIU: The second
automation I use is

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.180
for my open-source projects.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.450
So, for one of my libraries,
what I do now is every day,

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.220
I actually run a job
that just checks

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.290
for any open pull
requests and any issues.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.200
Spark can help us figure out
what are the duplicate issues,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.840
whether or not projects
are in the pipeline.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.360
And then for any pull requests,
it'll actually pull them down,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.300
review the code, look at the
comments, and just fix them

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.410
and push them back up.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.700
That way, anything
that I'm working

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.560
on is already fully tested.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.520
And for the third example,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.820
a place where Spark really
shines is in interactive coding.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.580
Oftentimes, when I know what
I want to do and I use Codex,

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.010
tasks might take
five or 10 minutes.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.570
This takes me out
of the flow state.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.010
The joy of using Spark is

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.510
that things are
incredibly interactive.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.830
Now, let's jump in
and see an example

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.870
of how interactive Spark can be.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.420
One of the benefits of using
a model that is so fast is

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.520
that when you're building on
an application that reloads

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.040
when the code changes,
oftentimes you can build

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.930
in a pretty real-time way.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.130
Let's take a look at an example.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.770
Can you take a look at
the homepage and add a

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.300
"See More" link to
the bottom right?

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.780
The bottom right of
every single cell.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.100
Then I would love for you to add
a little arrow after "See More"

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.630
so people know
that it is a link.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.190
Make sure it's blue
with no underline.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.770
Can you also make sure that the
first blog post is actually a

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:38.640
featured section, and then
we go to recent writing?

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.010
And then can you delete all
of the three posts to explore

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.480
and enough structure,
all that stuff?

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.530
You can delete all that stuff.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.950
That's perfect.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.570
SARAH CHIENG: Speed
is a major unlock.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.400
Those were just a few
ways to take advantage

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.250
of Spark inside the Codex app,
but there are dozens more.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.130
The point is, once you have a
model that responds instantly,

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.950
you stop thinking of it as a
tool you occasionally query

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:08.970
and start thinking of it

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.470
as infrastructure
that's always running.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:11.760
JASON LIU: Here's what
I think about it.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.920
The slower, smarter
models, full Codex,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.480
is like a bus for the mind.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.090
You can get on, fall asleep,
wake up, and you're there.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.260
It did all the work.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.970
Spark is like a fast car.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.440
You might have to pay
attention to the road,

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.960
but you can get there way faster
if you know what you're doing.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.090
But here's the thing I want
developers to understand.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.350
This is just v1.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.270
Our models on Cerebras
are going to get smarter.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:31.650
SARAH CHIENG: Which
means the tasks

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.230
that are Codex-only today
might be Spark tomorrow.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.410
So learn the tools now, because
when Spark v2, v3 land --

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.520
JASON LIU: You're going to
want to know how to use them.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.570
[ Music ]
